{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"1d5051a57c184937732c9067dee36ed8fcf0729a8ea33ff9dacb4607ffc4bfc5","slug":"midlatitude-cyclone-cyclogenesis","location":"/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_cyclogenesis.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Cyclogenesis Stage","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Kevin Goebbert","given":"Kevin","family":"Goebbert"},"name":"Kevin Goebbert","affiliations":["University of Tampa"],"id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","note":"All non-code content is licensed under CC-BY-4.0","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","note":"All code content is licensed under Apache-2.0","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook","edit_url":null,"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":false,"offset":2},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":true,"continue":true}},"keywords":["metpy","python","meteorology","weather"],"copyright":"2026","affiliations":[{"id":"University of Tampa","name":"University of Tampa"}],"settings":{"output_stderr":"remove-warn","output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook/blob/main/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_cyclogenesis.md","thumbnail":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlat_cyclone_cyclo-29e5b7fb9067d9e9c5daf374aeeb81f4.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"midlatitude_cyclone_cyclogenesis.md","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlatitude_cyclone_-3753cc2419dff6fb6548e1690d877214.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For cyclogenesis to occur at the surface, a wave in the upper-levels\nmust come along to provide the necessary support to strengthen the\nlow-level temperature gradient. This stage is signified by an increasing\ntemperature gradient, the beginning of cyclonic flow at the surface, and\na small decrease in the mean sea-level pressure\n(","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"CIo9Ee3ua1"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"cyclogenesis","label":"cyclogenesis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 26","key":"CiQWT1Rx2F"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"26","resolved":true,"html_id":"cyclogenesis","key":"N28vLADXwo"},{"type":"text","value":"). There are now\nfronts defined with a warm front typically east of the cyclone center\nand a cold front south and west of the cyclone center. The pressure\ntroughs in association with the fronts begin to develop.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"KS1oITmhgC"}],"key":"aoz4vpO8jq"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlat_cyclone_cyclo-29e5b7fb9067d9e9c5daf374aeeb81f4.png","alt":"Cyclogenesis stage of a Mid-latitude Cyclone","width":"650px","align":"center","key":"u5Ucne9l13","urlSource":"../../images/midlat_cyclone_cyclogenesis.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"cyclogenesis","identifier":"cyclogenesis","html_id":"cyclogenesis","enumerator":"26","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure 26:","key":"LliQ6g90I9"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"Y2Fv01tix6"},{"type":"text","value":"A schematic diagram of a surface cyclone during the cyclogenesis\nstage of development at the surface and 500 hPa with location of\nlow-level fronts for spatial reference.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"PQS2nrKJ5u"}],"key":"B3YtchteN8"}],"key":"ogQwKbDk4L"}],"label":"cyclogenesis","identifier":"cyclogenesis","enumerator":"26","html_id":"cyclogenesis","key":"McKSBueW90"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As the initial cyclone begins to develop, the trough begins to intensify\nsimultaneously. The intensification results from the different\ntemperature advection surrounding the mid-latitude cyclone. The 500-hPa\npattern strengthens (","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"e4JtB1gXRJ"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"cyclogenesis","label":"cyclogenesis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 26","key":"nEaEtyHY7a"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"26","resolved":true,"html_id":"cyclogenesis","key":"p1kRJ3rjkJ"},{"type":"text","value":") along\nwith the surface cyclone and is evidenced by the deepening of the\nupper-level trough. Additionally, a typical mid-latitude cyclone\nexhibits a tilt with height. The typical tilt is where the upper-level\ntrough (e.g., the 500-hPa trough) is located ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"OZOiOlQ9rK"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"to the west","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"Oy6gh4OYNB"}],"key":"f2agEwD6aw"},{"type":"text","value":" of the\nsurface mid-latitude cyclone (","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"fapyT2sBX4"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"cyclogenesis","label":"cyclogenesis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 26","key":"AstgzzIXGs"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"26","resolved":true,"html_id":"cyclogenesis","key":"YximuCtBhE"},{"type":"text","value":").\nThis ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"mJrjIxQUzS"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"westward tilt","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"hLt6dbO2oH"}],"key":"tx5AScEdGZ"},{"type":"text","value":" with height is essential to the continued\nstrengthening of the mid-latitude cyclone.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"FEk7ljfp4m"}],"key":"sNttpyHo15"}],"key":"KNvbbE5YRI"}],"key":"YI6xWVorjq"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Pre-Environment Stage","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-pre-environment","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Mature Stage","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}